WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:12.075
Go applications commonly receive configuration through environment variables, configuration files, command-line flags, runtime secret providers, or platform injection mechanisms.

2
00:00:12.075 --> 00:00:20.376
Each method can be reasonable when it fits the deployment model. The important question is not which mechanism sounds most modern.

3
00:00:20.376 --> 00:00:29.131
The important question is whether required secrets are loaded deliberately, validated clearly, and kept out of logs and error messages.

4
00:00:29.131 --> 00:00:36.074
Environment variables are common for containerized services because platforms can inject them at runtime.

5
00:00:36.074 --> 00:00:39.017
They are simple to read, but they are not magic.

6
00:00:39.017 --> 00:00:47.545
Environment variables may be visible to process inspection tools, crash reports, or debugging output depending on the environment.

7
00:00:47.545 --> 00:00:49.809
They also need careful validation.

8
00:00:49.809 --> 00:00:59.016
In Go, os.Getenv returns an empty string when a variable is unset and also returns an empty string when the variable is intentionally set to empty.

9
00:00:59.016 --> 00:01:05.808
For required values, os.LookupEnv is often safer because it tells you whether the variable existed at all.

10
00:01:05.808 --> 00:01:10.260
A small configuration loader can make startup behavior predictable.

11
00:01:10.260 --> 00:01:24.297
For example, a LoadConfig function might call os.LookupEnv for DB_PASSWORD and API_TOKEN, check that each required value is present, and return an error that says DB_PASSWORD is required without printing the password.

12
00:01:24.297 --> 00:01:27.920
The code should not log the full config struct on failure.

13
00:01:27.920 --> 00:01:40.598
It should fail closed, meaning the service refuses to start when a production secret is missing instead of silently switching to a default password, anonymous mode, or an unprotected local fallback.

14
00:01:40.598 --> 00:01:48.522
Configuration files can also work, especially for local development, but they need access controls and commit protection.

15
00:01:48.522 --> 00:01:56.447
A .env-like file may be acceptable for a developer workstation if it is ignored by git and contains only development secrets.

16
00:01:56.447 --> 00:02:00.446
It should not become a hidden path for production credentials.

17
00:02:00.446 --> 00:02:10.484
Command-line flags are useful for nonsecret settings, but secrets on command lines may appear in shell history or process listings, so use them with caution.

18
00:02:10.484 --> 00:02:18.634
Runtime providers, such as a managed secrets service or platform key vault, move secret storage out of the application package.

19
00:02:18.634 --> 00:02:28.218
They also introduce dependency behavior: authentication, network timeouts, retries, and failure modes. Your Go code still needs clear boundaries.

20
00:02:28.218 --> 00:02:39.086
Load what is required, validate what you received, pass values only to the components that need them, and report configuration problems without exposing sensitive data.

21
00:02:39.086 --> 00:02:46.406
A boring configuration loader that fails clearly is one of the strongest foundations for safe secrets management.

22
00:02:46.406 --> 00:02:50.481
Keep the loader small enough that reviewers can reason about it.

23
00:02:50.481 --> 00:02:59.387
A sprawling configuration package with reflection, implicit defaults, and mixed production and development behavior is harder to trust.

24
00:02:59.387 --> 00:03:07.537
A simple loader that names required settings, returns ordinary Go errors, and constructs a typed Config value is easier to test.

25
00:03:07.537 --> 00:03:13.424
It also gives operations a clear failure mode when deployment configuration is incomplete.
